    label: "The Day That Was",
    kicker: "Daily Wrap",
    headline: "Nifty adds 150 points to 23,961 and closes on the high for a fifth straight day as Media jumps 5.15% and mid and small caps finally outrun the index — IT is the only loser at -1.50%, with TCS down 2.52%",
    summary: "A fifth consecutive gain, and the widest one underneath. The Nifty 50 closed at its session high of 23,960.60, up 149.75 points or 0.63%, and the Sensex at 77,301.40, up 383.55 points or 0.50% and its highest close in more than two months. Both had been down in the morning — the Sensex fell as much as 313 points to 76,604.85 and the Nifty to 23,696.15 — before foreign inflows, Brent at $102.80 and a pullback in US Treasury yields turned the tape. What made this different from Tuesday is who led: Nifty Media +5.15% to 1,710.65, Realty +2.85%, Metal +2.80%, Consumer Durables +2.04%, Oil & Gas +1.54%, FMCG +1.50%, Auto +1.16% and Bank +0.67% to 55,913.60, against IT -1.50%, the only sector in the red. Media was a Saregama day: the stock rose 14.91% on a 47.62% jump in September-quarter net profit to Rs 64.60 crore, with Zee Entertainment +10.6%, Prime Focus +6.29%, Nazara +5.7% and DB Corp +4.1% following. The other difference is the breadth. The Midcap 150 rose 1.60% and the Smallcap 250 1.97%, both ahead of the Nifty for the first time this week, NSE breadth was 2,720 advances against 884 declines, and the Nifty 500 closed 408 up and 86 down. That is the five-day rally finally widening out from index weights. The one place it did not: IT, where TCS fell 2.52%, HCLTech 2.37%, Infosys 2.89% and Wipro 2.35% as the global AI-infrastructure trade unwound and Asian chip stocks tumbled. India VIX fell 3.24% to 15.21, FIIs had bought Rs 6,836.81 crore of equities on Tuesday while DIIs sold Rs 3,678.85 crore, and the day's politics — Democrats taking both chambers of the US Congress — was read as a risk to AI capex rather than to Indian earnings.",
